<header><p class="enc-kicker">Historical factor</p><h1 class="enc-title">Slavery and the slave trade</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> refers to the recurring pattern in which empires relied on enslaved labour and on capturing and trading people for wealth and power. Slaves worked plantations, mines and households, served as soldiers and were sold across deserts and oceans. Examples include the Portuguese and Brazilian empires in the Atlantic system, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo Empire</a> in the African trade and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>.</p></div>
